// U7 Final L1

::U7 Final L1 Q01::A supplement seller cites only customer testimonials. What is the strongest evaluation? {=Testimonials cannot establish cause; seek independent controlled evidence.~Testimonials prove effectiveness when many customers agree.~The claim is true because the product is sold publicly.~Price is the only evidence needed.}

::U7 Final L1 Q02::What does “trace a claim” require? {=Locate the original study, data, or statement and inspect its context.~Read only summaries that repeat the claim.~Count social-media likes.~Ask whether the headline is memorable.}

::U7 Final L1 Q03::A current government page and a ten-year-old anonymous blog disagree. Which factor most strongly favors the government page? {=Accountable authorship, current evidence, and transparent sourcing.~Its web address is shorter.~It contains more images.~It appears first in one search.}

::U7 Final L1 Q04::Why does a seller-funded study require additional scrutiny? {=A financial conflict may shape study design or reporting.~Funded research is always false.~Only free products can be studied fairly.~Funding matters more than methods in every case.}

::U7 Final L1 Q05::Two independent systematic reviews reach similar conclusions. What has increased? {=Corroboration of the conclusion.~Proof that no exceptions exist.~The popularity of the claim only.~The seller’s qualifications.}

::U7 Final L1 Q06::An app requests location and contact access unrelated to its function. What should a user evaluate? {=Whether the data collection is necessary, secure, and explained.~Whether the icon is attractive.~Whether friends installed it.~Whether it sends frequent notifications.}

::U7 Final L1 Q07::A provider has a license but advertises treatment outside that license. Best response? {=Verify scope of practice and seek an appropriately qualified provider.~Assume any license covers every treatment.~Judge only by the office design.~Use the service because it is nearby.}

::U7 Final L1 Q08::A headline says a food “cuts risk by 50%,” from 2 cases to 1 in 10,000. What is missing? {=The absolute risk, which shows the size of the change.~The product color.~A celebrity endorsement.~The number of headline words.}

// U7 Final L2

::U7 Final L2 Q01::Neighborhood B has a higher asthma-visit rate and more traffic. What may be concluded? {=There is a pattern worth investigating, but the data alone do not prove traffic caused it.~Traffic definitely caused every visit.~The neighborhoods are identical.~Rates cannot be compared.}

::U7 Final L2 Q02::Why use a rate rather than a raw count? {=A rate accounts for population size or time and permits fairer comparison.~A rate always proves causation.~Raw counts are never useful.~Rates remove all bias.}

::U7 Final L2 Q03::Which is a community health determinant? {=Reliable transportation to health services.~A person’s favorite color.~The title of a survey.~A random password.}

::U7 Final L2 Q04::A park plan improves access but removes a bus stop. What should planners examine? {=Unintended effects on people who depend on transit.~Only the opening-day attendance.~Whether the plan has a catchy name.~Only the construction color.}

::U7 Final L2 Q05::Which action best supports an equity analysis? {=Compare who benefits, who bears burdens, and who faces access barriers.~Assume one solution affects everyone equally.~Exclude affected residents from planning.~Use averages only.}

::U7 Final L2 Q06::Air readings were collected on one calm morning. What is a key limitation? {=One time point may not represent typical or peak exposure.~Air cannot be measured.~Morning data are automatically false.~Location never matters.}

::U7 Final L2 Q07::What best distinguishes correlation from causation? {=Correlation shows variables move together; causation requires evidence that one produces change in the other.~Correlation is stronger than experimental evidence.~They mean exactly the same thing.~Causation requires no mechanism.}

::U7 Final L2 Q08::Which measure best evaluates a new safe-routes program? {=Changes in route use and injury/near-miss data over a defined period.~The logo color.~One organizer’s opinion only.~Total city population.}

// U7 Final L3

::U7 Final L3 Q01::A campaign goal is “help everyone be healthier.” What is the main weakness? {=It lacks a specific audience, behavior, timeframe, and measure.~It is too measurable.~It identifies too many sources.~It contains too much accessibility information.}

::U7 Final L3 Q02::Which call to action is most usable? {=Refill your bottle at the station beside the library entrance today.~Care more about hydration.~Make better choices someday.~Everyone should change.}

::U7 Final L3 Q03::Why use plain language in health communication? {=It improves comprehension and action without sacrificing accuracy.~It guarantees agreement.~It removes the need for evidence.~It targets only young children.}

::U7 Final L3 Q04::A flyer uses tiny pale text. Which principle is violated? {=Accessibility.~Corroboration.~Random sampling.~Confidentiality of a dataset.}

::U7 Final L3 Q05::Which message avoids stigmatizing framing? {=Describe the barrier and available support without blaming affected people.~Label people by a health condition.~Use fear and shame to force action.~Assume behavior reflects character.}

::U7 Final L3 Q06::Why identify a decision-maker in advocacy? {=The requested change must reach someone able to authorize or influence it.~It makes evidence unnecessary.~Only elected officials make decisions.~It guarantees success.}

::U7 Final L3 Q07::What is the best evidence that a communication campaign worked? {=A predefined audience behavior or comprehension measure improved.~The designer likes the poster.~The message uses many colors.~The campaign was expensive.}

::U7 Final L3 Q08::A message says “studies prove” but lists no source. Best revision? {=Name and link the relevant credible evidence and represent its limits.~Add an exclamation point.~Remove all uncertainty words.~Replace evidence with a testimonial.}

// U7 Final L4

::U7 Final L4 Q01::Which goal is SMART? {=For four weeks, average 7.5 hours of sleep on school nights using a nightly log.~Sleep more eventually.~Become perfectly healthy.~Never feel tired again.}

::U7 Final L4 Q02::Why record a baseline? {=It provides a starting point for judging change.~It guarantees improvement.~It replaces a goal.~It hides variation.}

::U7 Final L4 Q03::What is an adaptation trigger? {=A predefined condition that signals the plan should be reviewed or changed.~A punishment for missing one day.~A goal with no measure.~A reason to abandon monitoring.}

::U7 Final L4 Q04::Why consider nine wellness domains? {=Domains interact, so a plan should anticipate benefits and tradeoffs.~Every domain must receive identical time.~Only physical wellness can be measured.~Domains never affect one another.}

::U7 Final L4 Q05::A learner misses one planned walk. Best response? {=Review the broader pattern before deciding whether the plan needs revision.~Declare the entire plan failed.~Double every goal immediately.~Stop recording data.}

::U7 Final L4 Q06::Which indicator best matches a social-wellness goal? {=Number of planned supportive contacts completed each week.~Daily temperature outside.~Price of groceries only.~Hours of unrelated screen time.}

::U7 Final L4 Q07::A goal depends on equipment the learner cannot access. What should change? {=Revise the action step to an accessible alternative while preserving the purpose.~Keep the impossible action unchanged.~Remove all monitoring.~Blame motivation.}

::U7 Final L4 Q08::Why schedule review dates? {=Regular review turns observations into timely, evidence-based adjustments.~Review dates guarantee perfect results.~Plans should be changed every day.~Only final outcomes matter.}

// U7 Final L5

::U7 Final L5 Q01::A new evening job conflicts with an exercise schedule. Best adaptation? {=Preserve the goal but revise timing and supports using the new schedule evidence.~Abandon wellness permanently.~Ignore the work schedule.~Increase the goal without changing the plan.}

::U7 Final L5 Q02::What should remain continuous across a transition? {=Core purpose and effective protective factors, when still relevant.~Every exact action regardless of circumstances.~Every baseline value.~All barriers.}

::U7 Final L5 Q03::Which is a protective factor during relocation? {=A planned way to maintain supportive relationships.~No access to information.~An unmeasurable goal.~Avoiding all reassessment.}

::U7 Final L5 Q04::Why reassess after a major life change? {=Needs, resources, risks, and routines may have changed.~Previous goals become automatically false.~Monitoring is no longer useful.~Every domain must be abandoned.}

::U7 Final L5 Q05::Which contingency is specific and useful? {=If travel prevents gym access for two weeks, use three 20-minute bodyweight sessions.~Try harder if anything changes.~Never alter the plan.~Wait indefinitely without monitoring.}

::U7 Final L5 Q06::What makes a wellness strategy sustainable? {=It fits available resources, can be monitored, and can adapt over time.~It requires maximum effort every day.~It depends on one unavailable resource.~It has no review process.}

::U7 Final L5 Q07::How should a capstone address two life-stage scenarios? {=Identify changed conditions, affected domains, preserved goals, adaptations, and indicators.~List transitions without changing the plan.~Predict exact future outcomes.~Use private disclosures only.}

::U7 Final L5 Q08::Why evaluate resource credibility in a lifetime plan? {=Future decisions should rely on current, qualified, evidence-based guidance.~Popular sources never change.~Credibility matters only once.~All sources provide equal support.}
